---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeamRail from '../components/SeamRail.astro';
import BrandDevice from '../components/BrandDevice.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Approach"
  description="The InclusionEdge approach: demand-first job deconstruction across physical, cognitive, and sensory domains — delivered by one practitioner, verified by one evidence standard."
>
  <section class="page-hero surface-navy">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Approach</span>
      <h1>We start with the work, not the worker.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Most inclusion training starts with awareness — here is what neurodivergence is, here is how to be inclusive. We start with the task. We decompose roles into their physical, cognitive, and sensory demands. Then we match capability to demand. The gap is what we address — not the label, not the diagnosis, the specific demand. One practitioner holds the full arc from that first analysis through to proof. No handoffs. No unverified claims. No workshop that evaporates the moment it ends.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Demand-first analysis -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">How we deconstruct the work</span>
        <h2>Every task has demands. Most of them are invisible.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Before we design a single module, we decompose the roles in question into their physical, cognitive, and sensory demands. Most inclusion training starts with awareness — here is what neurodivergence is, here is how to be inclusive. We start with the task: what does this role actually ask someone to do, under what conditions, and where are the mismatches? A role stops being a job description and becomes a set of specific, visible demands — anyone can see them, anyone can redesign them. This is what turns inclusion from a values conversation into an engineering standard.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <div class="method-card">
          <span class="method-label">Physical demands</span>
          <h3>What the body is asked to do</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Movement, coordination, stamina, sensory exposure. What does the role actually require the body to do, under what conditions?
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-card">
          <span class="method-label">Cognitive demands</span>
          <h3>What the mind is asked to hold</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Memory, sequencing, processing speed, ambiguity tolerance. Where does the role ask someone to hold multiple things in their head at once? Where does it assume a particular communication style?
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-card">
          <span class="method-label">Sensory demands</span>
          <h3>What the environment imposes</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Noise, light, interruptions, visual density. What does the environment impose before any interpersonal behaviour even enters the picture?
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pull-quote">
        The mismatch is not in the person. It is between what the task demands and what the conditions allow.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Support architecture -->
  <section class="section surface-tint">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="spine">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">How we sustain</span>
          <h2>We leave. The architecture stays.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Most inclusion consultancies are built on retention — the programme depends on the provider coming back. We build the opposite: a support architecture your organisation owns and runs. A supervisor-champion who holds the behavioural standard. A deputy who ensures continuity. A co-worker who handles the everyday moment. Our job is to equip them, embed the system, and step back. The starting point — which job, which team, at what pace — is yours.
          </p>
          <p>
            CARE&trade; and ENGAGE&trade; are designed for this transfer. CARE&trade; equips every staff member with what to say and do in the moment — so the co-worker is not guessing. ENGAGE&trade; trains managers to own and model the standard — so the champion and deputy are not improvising. The training is not the endpoint. It is the handover.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="spine-layers">
          <div class="layer layer-deep">
            <span class="layer-tag">Ownership</span>
            <h3>Supervisor-champion</h3>
            <p class="text-small text-muted">
              The manager who owns the behavioural standard for their team — trained through ENGAGE&trade;, equipped to sustain it, and accountable for it after we step back.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="layer">
            <span class="layer-tag">Continuity</span>
            <h3>Deputy</h3>
            <p class="text-small text-muted">
              The second-line support who holds the standard when the champion is absent — so continuity does not depend on one person, or on us.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="layer">
            <span class="layer-tag">Peer support</span>
            <h3>Co-worker</h3>
            <p class="text-small text-muted">
              The peer-level support who handles the everyday moment — the question in the hallway, the check-in after a difficult meeting. Equipped through CARE&trade;, because the person closest to the work is the person who sustains it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Forward-deployed delivery -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">How we deliver</span>
        <h2>One practitioner. The full causal chain. No handoffs.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Most providers hand off between sales, design, delivery, and measurement. Every handoff introduces translation loss — the degradation of intent across organisational layers — and translation loss is where inclusion programmes quietly fail. One practitioner runs your engagement from discovery through to proof, supported by infrastructure that absorbs the work a delivery team would otherwise carry.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rail-legend">
        <span><i class="dot dot-visible" aria-hidden="true"></i>What you experience</span>
        <span><i class="dot dot-seam" aria-hidden="true"></i>What runs in the seam</span>
      </div>

      <SeamRail />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Assurance -->
  <section class="section surface-navy">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="evidence">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">Where we're grounded</span>
          <h2>We built the method to be ready for an inclusive workforce.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Our approach is aligned with international best practice in inclusive employment and with Singapore's national direction — the Enabling Masterplan 2030, the Workplace Fairness Act, the Enabling Skills and Competencies Framework, and the SkillsFuture national movement. These are not abstract commitments. Each one informs a specific part of how we work: what we measure, how we design for transfer, and why the support architecture matters.
          </p>
          <p class="lede">
            We did not wait for the Workplace Fairness Act to arrive before building a method that holds managers accountable for behavioural standards. We did not wait for the Enabling Masterplan to set its targets before designing a delivery model that turns assessment into participation. The national agenda is catching up to where the evidence has been pointing for years. Our job is to operationalise it — in your teams, with your work, at your pace.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="evidence-panel">
          <h3>What grounds us</h3>
          <ul class="source-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Enabling Masterplan 2030</strong>
              <span>National direction on disability inclusion and employment participation — the &ldquo;why&rdquo; behind every engagement.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>SG Enable &amp; ESCF</strong>
              <span>The Enabling Skills and Competencies Framework — 50 competencies that inform how we design for capability, not just awareness.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Workplace Fairness Act</strong>
              <span>Statutory protection against workplace discrimination — the compliance horizon our support architecture is built to meet.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Skills and Workforce Development Agency (SWDA)</strong>
              <span>National skills and workforce development movement — our training maps to WSQ and SkillsFuture competencies where applicable.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>International evidence base</strong>
              <span>Dobbin &amp; Kalev, Kirkpatrick Levels 3&ndash;4, and the broader evidence on what actually changes workplace behaviour — the research that shaped the method.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-sm cta-band">
    <div class="container cta-inner">
      <BrandDevice variant="seam" class="cta-device" />
      <h2>Enough reading. See the instruments.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The showcase illustrates the diagnostic, learning and measurement approach we use in our engagements.
      </p>
      <a href="/showcase/" class="btn btn-primary">Open the showcase</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6.5rem); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 18ch; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .page-hero .lede { max-width: 56ch; }

  .method-card {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--navy);
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .method-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--teal-deep);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
  }

  .method-card h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .method-card p { font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0; }

  .spine {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .spine h2 { margin-bottom: 1.15rem; }
  .spine .lede { margin-bottom: 1.15rem; }

  .spine-layers { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }

  .layer {
    background: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--gray-400);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.35rem 1.5rem;
  }
  .layer-deep { border-left-color: var(--teal); }

  .layer-tag {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-micro);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gray-600);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .layer-deep .layer-tag { color: var(--teal-deep); }
  .layer h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .layer p { margin: 0; }

  .rail-legend {
    display: flex;
    gap: 2rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--gray-600);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .dot {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    margin-right: 0.55rem;
    vertical-align: 0;
  }
  .dot-visible { background: var(--white); border: 1.5px solid var(--gray-400); }
  .dot-seam { background: var(--teal-tint); border: 1.5px dashed var(--teal); }

  .evidence {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 0.9fr;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .evidence h2 { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  .evidence .lede { margin-bottom: 1.15rem; }

  .evidence-panel {
    background: var(--navy-800);
    border: 1px solid var(--navy-700);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 2rem;
  }
  .evidence-panel h3 {
    font-size: var(--fs-micro);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--teal);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .source-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .source-list li {
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-dark);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .source-list li:first-child { border-top: none; padding-top: 0; }
  .source-list strong {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--white);
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }
  .source-list span { font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--gray-400); line-height: 1.55; }

  .cta-band { background: var(--gray-100); border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .cta-inner { text-align: center; max-width: 44rem; }
  .cta-device {
    width: 180px;
    margin: 0 auto 1.75rem;
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .cta-inner h2 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .cta-inner .lede { margin-bottom: 2rem; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .spine, .evidence { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
